<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · concept</p><h1 class="enc-title">Foot binding</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Footbinding, Chánzú, Lotus feet</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Foot binding was the Chinese custom of breaking and tightly binding the feet of young girls to change their shape and size, a painful practice that limited women's mobility and left lifelong disabilities. It may have begun among court dancers in the 10th century and spread among the elite under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song dynasty</a>, the earliest archaeological evidence coming from tombs of 1243 and 1274. It later spread to lower classes, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manchus">Manchu</a> bans of 1636 to 1664 failed. In late imperial <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> bound feet were a status symbol and a mark of beauty; by the 19th century perhaps 40 to 50% of Chinese women had them. Reformers and missionaries campaigned against it, and it died out in the early 20th century.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/foot-binding.jpg" alt="Foot binding" width="330" height="456" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Lai Afong · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:A_Chinese_Golden_Lily_Foot,_Lai_Afong,_c1870s.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Several stories tell of foot binding before its establishment under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song dynasty</a>. One concerns Pan Yunu, a consort of the Southern Qi emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xiao-baojuan">Xiao Baojuan</a>, whose dancing was said to make lotus flowers spring from her steps, though there is no evidence that she bound her feet. Another links the custom to the 10th-century emperor Li Yu of the Southern Tang, who asked his concubine Yao Niang to bind her feet in white <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">silk</a> and dance on a golden lotus; others, it was said, then imitated her.</p><p>Admiration for small feet existed earlier: a Tang tale of about 850, that of Ye Xian, contains elements of the Cinderella story. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odoric-of-pordenone">Odoric of Pordenone</a> mentioned foot binding in the 14th century, but <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marco-polo">Marco Polo</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-battuta">Ibn Battuta</a> did not, which may show that it was not yet widespread. By the Ming period it was a status symbol, and its rise coincided with a decline of women's dance.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>Binding began before the arch had fully developed, usually between the ages of four and nine, often in winter, when numb feet made the pain less sharp. The foot was soaked in a mixture of herbs and animal blood, the toenails cut back, and the toes curled under and pressed into the sole until they broke. Long <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a> bandages were wound in a figure of eight and tightened with each pass, forcing the arch to break and the heel and ball of the foot together.</p><p>Practice varied by region and class. Infection was the commonest problem, and poor circulation left injuries unhealed. It is thought that as many as 10 per cent of girls may have died of gangrene and other infections. Frontier peoples such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uyghurs">Uyghurs</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manchus">Manchus</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibetans">Tibetans</a> generally did not practise it.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>Interpretations differ. Some see fashion and beauty, since a woman with small lotus feet could marry more prestigiously; others point to the seclusion of women, or to a wish to keep daughters at handwork. Laurel Bossen and Hill Gates reject the idea that it was thought more beautiful or won better marriages.</p><p>Some Chinese writers opposed the custom in the 18th century, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taiping-rebellion">Taiping</a> leaders outlawed it where they ruled. Christian missionaries and Chinese reformers, among them the early feminist Qiu Jin, campaigned against it in the late 19th century. It began to die out in the early 20th century, upper-class and urban women giving it up sooner than poorer rural women.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Foot binding” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Foot binding</b> became a new fashion among elite women in the Southern Song.</blockquote><a href="/societies/song-china">Social Hierarchy of Song China</a></li></ul></section>
